y shown)\n  ❌ \"k=X is achievable\" (no construction provided)\n  ❌ \"Construction can be found using vertical lines\" (method named but not executed)\n\n**Classification Rules:**\n- If solution claims \"k=X is achievable\" WITHOUT providing construction → **INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2**\n- If solution provides strategy OR explicit construction → **VALID METHOD → proceed to Level 3**\n\n**Valid justifications** (these are VALID METHODS):\n  ✅ \"Use lines x=1, x=2, ..., x=n\" (explicit specification)\n  ✅ \"L: y = mx + b where...\" (explicit equation)\n  ✅ \"Line through points (a,b) and (c,d)\" (strategy with specific values)\n\n**THIS IS A LEVEL 2 GATE CHECK, NOT LEVEL 3 PRESENTATION:**\n- Unjustified existence = logically incomplete = INVALID METHOD = FAIL Level 2\n- You MUST classify \"construction exists\" (without details) as INVALID_METHOD\n- This triggers Level 2 FAIL - do NOT proceed to Level 3\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.  Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through\nthe leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line\ncovers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the\nreplacement we still cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and we have exactly the same\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\nConsequently we may (and shall) assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the\nvertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nAll points with abscissa \\(a\\le n-k\\) are already covered.  The remaining\npoints are\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\na right‑angled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).  Thus the original\nproblem is equivalent to **Problem (*)** stated in the summary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a sunny line be given.  Multiplying its equation by a common denominator we\nobtain an integer equation\n\n\\[\np x-q y=c ,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on the line are described by (3) above:\n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums satisfy (4):\n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\); therefore the sum changes by at least\n\\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) when \\(t\\) varies by one.\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  From (2.3) the\nnumber of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  The equality \\(p+q=1\\) forces\n\\(q\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(p=0\\) whi